    {
     "id": "s2_j81_i39_col16_enhancement_factor_at_its_clamp",
     "description": "Reference run: WRF 4.8 real-terrain simulation of data/eqwefic/notes/gwdo_real_reference_run.md (Last Chance Gulch d01, Colorado Rockies, 8.1 km, 150 x 180 x 50, dt = 45 s, gwd_opt = 1 with ELVMAX supplied by WPS util/compute_gwdo.py -- without it GWDO is silently off everywhere, FORTRAN_BUGS B17). Step 2 = 06-25 12:01Z, global row j = 81, tile column 16 of the dump that starts at i = 39. The CLAMP regime: the Froude number reaches 5.38, the largest in the dumped set, which drives the asymmetry enhancement (oa + 2)^(ce Fr) to 12.9 and pins it at efacmax = 10 -- the only branch of the launch block that the other regimes leave untested. The convexity factor is correspondingly saturated at 0.986 and the non-hydrostatic correction has almost cancelled the stress, 1 + nhd = 5.1e-3, leaving a launch stress of only 5e-4 Pa. An eight-layer blocking depth. Reference build: the real64 (kind_phys = real64) replay of the same dumped row through kernels/gwdo_diag_driver of WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-gwdo, with the six scalar constants g, cp, R_d, R_v, eps_1 and pi supplied at binary64 precision instead of WRF's real32 module values (gwdo_real/mkflat64.py). They are subroutine arguments, not internal parameters, so that is what a double-precision kernel reference means for this scheme, and it is exactly what lib/wrf_constants.esm carries; promoting them flips no branch on any of the 140 dumped rows and moves the outputs by a median 2.7e-8 of the row maximum. Every assertion is on an algebraic observed at t = 0 and the document declares no ODE state, so the empty time_span is a single static evaluation.",
